.TH CYAML_DICTIONARY_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_dictionary_set \- insert or replace a key in a dictionary
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_dictionary_set(cyaml map, const char *key,
                                cyaml child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Store child under key in the CYAML_DICTIONARY node map.  The dictionary
makes its own copy of key.  On success, ownership of child transfers to
map.  Do not free child after such a call.  When the key is already
present, the function deep-frees the previous child and then stores the
new child in the same slot.  Nothing between the two steps can fail, so
the slot never names freed memory once the call returns.  The key keeps
its place in the member order of the dictionary.  A new key goes after
every member that the dictionary already holds; see
cyaml_dictionary_first(3).

One return code carries one rule about child, exactly as on
cyaml_list_push(3).  ccol_invalid_args always means that the function
rejected the arguments and did not touch child at all.  The caller still
owns child and must destroy it itself.  Every other failure takes
ownership of child and deep-frees it before the function returns.  The
caller must not destroy child after any of those failures.

The function rejects these arguments with ccol_invalid_args.  They are a
NULL child, a NULL map, a NULL key, a key that is not well-formed
UTF-8, and a map that is not a CYAML_DICTIONARY.  It also rejects a child that is already attached to a
list parent or a dictionary parent.  It rejects a child that is map
itself.  The attachment rule has one exception.
You can set key to the value that it already holds.  The function
accepts the borrowed reference that this same slot owns.  It then does
nothing and returns ccol_success.

child must also not already hold map somewhere in its own subtree.  The
function does not check this.  A cycle that you make this way turns a
later cyaml_destroy(), cyaml_clone() or cyaml_serialize() call on the
tree into undefined behaviour.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
map    CYAML_DICTIONARY target node.
key    Null-terminated, well-formed UTF-8 key string.  Every Unicode
       character is accepted; the serializer escapes one that a
       YAML document may not hold raw.
child  Value node.  Ownership transfers, but not on ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full.
ccol_invalid_args leaves child untouched, and the caller still owns it.
It comes back when map, key or child is NULL.  It also comes back when
key is not well-formed UTF-8, when map is not a dictionary, when child is already attached, or when child
is map itself.
ccol_container_full means that the dictionary holds the largest element
count that it can represent.  A real program does not reach that count.
Ownership of child transfers on every outcome other than
ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_size (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_create_dictionary (3)
